@event art_escapement_drill
# en: The Ticking Hour
title [0f189f] = 滴答一课
# en: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == "winter": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}
text [d91972] = {奇械术从一颗心开始：一团发条，必须先走得均匀，才被允许思考。|你的发条心还在你留下它的地方，用错误的速度生着闷气。|如今你在门口就能听见自己工作台上那颗心。它的滴答声几乎就是你的。} {隔壁工作台滴答得像下冰雹。|门边有一颗心在倒着走，还挺得意。|讲师闭着眼，挨个工作台地听。} {season == "winter": 黄铜冷得咬手。|工坊里一股焊锡味。}
# en: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.
scene [2c43ab] = 杂乱的工坊长凳上堆满细小的黄铜齿轮、锉刀和放大镜，一排半成品的发条心各走各的节拍。
# en: File every tooth by hand
opt.left.label [93a8dc] = 一齿一齿手工锉
# en: {Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}
opt.left.say.1 [89f0bd] = {一齿接一齿，伏在放大镜下，直到眼睛发酸。下课铃响前，滴答声匀了。|你锉了一上午，还被焊锡烫了一下拇指。那颗心走得稳稳当当，还有点得意。|又慢又磨人的活儿，你的手比脑子学得快。}
# en: Charm the spring to keep time
opt.right.label [93d2cf] = 给发条施咒定时
# en: {The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.}
opt.right.say.1 [40de08] = {咒语钻进发条，像猫钻进篮子。你的心走得像节拍器一样准。|你把节奏低声说进发条圈里，它就守住了。讲师睁开一只眼，又闭上了。}
# en: {The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.}
opt.right.say.2 [8442a1] = {发条把咒语理解得太字面，一口气全松开，飞过了整间屋子。|你的心完美地走了一分钟，然后飞快，然后彻底停了。有人递给你一只灭火桶，以防万一。}

@event art_stirring_cauldron
# en: The Cauldron That Stirs
title [dc6732] = 自己搅拌的坩埚
# en: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}
text [9992ac] = {这门课的项目是一口会自己搅拌的坩埚。它确实会搅，大多数时候，朝它高兴的方向。|坩埚又被重装了一遍。新的黄铜搅臂，还是老脾气。|坩埚认得你，或者至少装得像认得。} {今天它先顺时针搅，然后开始闹别扭。|今天它搅得太快，锅里的粥都往外爬。|今天搅臂一个劲儿地敲锅沿，像个不耐烦的客人。} {final_year: 你会有点想念它的脾气。|门边的灭火桶难得都是满的。}
# en: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.
scene [9242ac] = 一口铁腿铜坩埚用一根长长的黄铜臂自己搅拌，学生们抱着笔记本围在一旁，小心地保持着距离。
# en: Recut the runes on the arm
opt.left.label [72bcaa] = 重刻搅臂上的符文
# en: {You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.}
opt.left.say.1 [3e8fc6] = {你拿着刻刀和放大镜过了一上午，一笔一画重刻搅拌符文。手指划了几道口子，搅臂动得顺滑了。|一整节课都在黄铜上做细活。搅臂搅得均匀了，你手上多了一排整整齐齐的焊锡烫痕。}
# en: Test it on a live brew
opt.right.label [4e638a] = 拿真药剂试一试
# en: {The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.}
opt.right.say.1 [9a1080] = {坩埚完全按配方搅好了你的药剂，两份功劳你都领了。|药剂在对的那一分钟变成了对的颜色。坩埚似乎知道自己干得不错。}
# en: {The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.}
opt.right.say.2 [4cc92a] = {搅臂越转越快，药剂爬上锅壁，全班齐刷刷后退一步。|坩埚把你的药剂搅成了一种像吵架一样的质地。你一直刷锅刷到下课。}

@event star_names_in_the_dark
# en: Names in the Dark
title [d9ffd3] = 黑暗中的名字
# en: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and this morning's note gives you tonight's hour. It begins with names. "You cannot call what you cannot name."|Another note at breakfast, another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|Tonight's appointment under the dome again. You know where the draught comes in.} {The note warns that the great telescope has been swinging round on its own.|Meteors are expected tonight.|The note is written on the back of a star chart.} {season == "winter": Tonight your breath will freeze on the eyepiece.|Tonight the air will smell of cut grass from the lawns below.}
text [3b909c] = {唤星术只在夜里预约授课，今早的字条告诉了你今晚的钟点。它从名字开始。“叫不出名字的，就唤不下来。”|早饭时又来了一张字条，又是一次穹顶下的夜间预约。名单比上次更长了。|今晚又是穹顶下的预约。你知道冷风从哪儿钻进来。} {字条提醒说，大望远镜最近总是自己转方向。|今晚会有流星。|字条写在一张星图的背面。} {season == "winter": 今晚你的呼气会冻在目镜上。|今晚的夜风里会有下面草坪刚割过的青草味。}
# en: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.
scene [383740] = 黄铜穹顶在黑暗中向满天星斗敞开，一名学生凑在大望远镜的目镜前，另一名呼着白气凭记忆背诵。
# en: Recite the names until perfect
opt.left.label [5a0fad] = 把名字背到一字不差
# en: {You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}
opt.left.say.1 [eebfcf] = {你对着寒气一遍遍念，直到它们不再是词，而成了地方。|一个接一个，正着背，再倒着背。到最后你闭着眼也能找到它们，也差点真得闭着眼。|同一个名字你卡了四次。第五次终于念对，你感觉它落在了某个地方。}
# en: Let the telescope choose a star
opt.right.label [9ee4f3] = 让望远镜挑一颗星
# en: {The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.}
opt.right.say.1 [d404ef] = {望远镜停在一颗你从没听说过的星上。讲师还没开口，你已经在旧星图里找到了它的名字。|它指哪儿你跟哪儿，星图也都对得上。这一课是倒着学会的。}
# en: {The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.}
opt.right.say.2 [02f475] = {望远镜挑了一颗星，然后每十分钟改一次主意。你绕着穹顶追它追到午夜。|你在目镜前熬了一夜，下来时脖子僵了，还有一个拿不准的名字。}

@event star_thread_of_light
# en: A Thread of Light
title [af6199] = 一缕星光
# en: {Tonight's lesson, says the morning note, is to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|Tonight's lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. The morning note says tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {The morning sky is clear, and the night promises to be.|Thin cloud is forecast to cross the star you've chosen.|At breakfast the others are already practising the hand positions.}
text [49defa] = {早上的字条说，今晚这一课要试着唤下一缕某颗有名之星的光，细不过一根头发。|今晚这一课永远一样：一缕光，一颗星，能撑多久撑多久。|你以前唤下过光缕。早上的字条说，今晚讲师要你撑得更久。} {早晨的天空晴朗，夜里看来也会如此。|预报说薄云会掠过你选中的那颗星。|早饭时其他人已经在练手势了。}
# en: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.
scene [db7c41] = 昏暗的观星台里，一名学生双手向上合拢，一缕银色星光穿过敞开的穹顶落进掌心。
# en: Call the thread down yourself
opt.left.label [3c139b] = 亲手把光唤下来
# en: {The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}
opt.left.say.1 [63b5e3] = {光缕冰冷而明亮地落下，注满你的掌心。你一直撑到双手发抖。|试了三次。第三次，光像从极高处落下的水一样掉进你手里。|一根头发粗的光，有整座塔那么长。它消散后，你的指尖还亮了一个钟头。}
# en: Keep the night log instead
opt.right.label [314549] = 改去记夜间日志
# en: {You write down every thread the others call, with the star, the minute and how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.} {season == "winter": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}
opt.right.say.1 [cc2069] = {你记下别人唤下的每一缕光：哪颗星、几点几分、撑了多久。又冷又枯燥，但你开始看出规律了。|别人唤光时你举灯记录。从旁边看，你清清楚楚看到他们的手错在哪儿。} {season == "winter": 你的墨水一直结冰。|讲师把你的日志读了两遍。}
# en: Hold your own starlight up to it
opt.up.label [658616] = 举起自己的星光迎它
# en: {You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}
opt.up.say.1 [48b9e8] = {你点亮自己的星光，那缕光循着它而来，同类相召。它撑了两倍长。|你的星光稳住了光缕，讲师让你示范给其他人看。|光缕径直落进你手中已有的光里。那一刻，穹顶亮如正午。}

@event hollow_sealed_boxes
# en: How It Was Sealed
title [e1eab1] = 它是怎么封上的
# en: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. "Tell me how it was closed," she says. "Not what is inside. How."|Another sealed thing on the table. Today it is {a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places today's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}
text [e8bcf8] = {每一课，院长都把一样封住的东西放到你面前。“告诉我它是怎么封上的，”她说，“不是里面有什么。是怎么封的。”|桌上又是一样封住的东西。今天是{一封盖着蜡印的信|一只小漆盒|一个盖子被咒语封死的罐子}。|院长带着一丝微笑放下今天的封物。她已经开始挑更难的了。} {油灯嘶嘶作响。|书架上的书在黑暗里缓缓呼吸。|她双手交叠坐着，不慌不忙。}
# en: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.
scene [8b6e1a] = 一间没有窗户的房间，四壁摆满黑色封皮的书，桌上一盏孤灯，一位高挑沉静的女士把一只上锁的小盒子放在学生面前。
# en: Study the seal for the hour
opt.left.label [b8b4fc] = 用整节课研究封印
# en: {You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}
opt.left.say.1 [af3511] = {你不碰它，只把每一道纹路都描了一遍。最后你能按顺序说出封印的每一步。她点了一下头。|花了整整一节课和一场头痛，你一层一层倒推了出来。|你对了两层，错了一层。她温和地告诉你是哪一层，你会记住的。}
# en: Open it without leaving a trace
opt.right.label [ffc168] = 打开它，不留痕迹
# en: {You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. "Good," she says. "Now never do that to a door."}
opt.right.say.1 [544bb6] = {你打开又合上，什么也看不出来。她看看它，又看看你，短促而愉快地笑了一声。|开了，合了，一丝痕迹也没有。“很好，”她说，“以后永远别对一扇门这么做。”}
# en: {The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. "Everyone breaks one," she says pleasantly. "Rarely so loudly."}
opt.right.say.2 [5226db] = {封印发出一声细小而决绝的脆响。院长在本子上写了点什么。她记得每一个错误，现在又多了一个。|开是开了，痕迹也露了。“每个人都会弄坏一个，”她和颜悦色地说，“很少有人弄得这么响。”}

@event hollow_reading_the_blank
# en: Reading the Blank Page
title [1973a9] = 读空白的一页
# en: {"Most things that are hidden are not gone," the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. "Find what was written. Gently."|A new blank page today. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}
text [747881] = {“大多数被藏起来的东西并没有消失，”院长说着，递给你一张白纸，“找出写过的东西。轻一点。”|今天又是一张新的白纸。它比纸该有的温度要暖。|如今你能掂出一页纸里藏着字的分量，就像口袋里装着东西。} {最近那排书架上的书似乎凑近了些。|油灯烧得又低又蓝。|书库深处，有本书自己翻了一页。} {final_year: 她问你毕业后要去哪里，然后认真听着。|她在你干活时看书，头也不抬。}
# en: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.
scene [6dbe72] = 一名学生借着灯光俯身看一张看似空白的纸，淡淡的墨迹在指尖下浮现，黑色封皮的书从书架上倾身过来。
# en: Coax the ink up slowly
opt.left.label [a0bd04] = 慢慢把墨迹引出来
# en: {You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull. It's someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}
opt.left.say.1 [ea3c8e] = {你用一缕法力一口气一口气地温着纸页。字迹慢慢浮上来，安全又无聊：某人的洗衣清单，藏得非常仔细。|墨迹一行一行浮现，是一张旧课表。这一课的意义在于找到它。|这几乎耗尽了你的力气。纸页只吐出一句话，讲的是几十年前的天气。}
# en: Read it bare-handed
opt.right.label [de8da5] = 徒手直接读
# en: {The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.}
opt.right.say.1 [9cb0f4] = {墨迹在你指下飞快浮现，一股寒意也一起涌上来，在你手里一直待到早上。|你读得又快又好。指尖疼了一个钟头，院长一言不发地递给你一杯茶。}
# en: Let your ward do the reading
opt.up.label [3adba1] = 让护界替你读
# en: {Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}
opt.up.say.1 [50deed] = {你的护界像一口屏住的气一样罩在纸页上，字迹浮进其中。院长扬起眉毛，颇为满意。|你隔着护界读，像隔着玻璃。它耗你的力，但不伤你的手。|藏起的字句在你的护壳里一行行浮现，不慌不忙。}
